        <p>{"Upstream covers the raw materials and packaging that enter a beverage plant. Water is the single most important input — carbonated water averages ~94% of a soft drink's volume — and must be treated to strict purity standards (ChangeLab Solutions, 2011). Sweeteners come next: cane or beet sugar, high-fructose corn syrup, and increasingly stevia or aspartame for sugar-free lines, typically 6-12% of a soft drink. Other inputs include flavouring agents, CO2 for carbonation, caffeine, vitamins, and fruit concentrates. Packaging is a major cost block — PET bottles, aluminium cans, glass and Tetra Pak cartons together typically run ~25-35% of cost of goods, with PET the cheapest, aluminium cans in the middle, and glass the most expensive (International Aluminium Institute, 2024). Vietnam is a net sugar importer: in one recent year it exported ~250,000 tonnes but imported ~640,000 tonnes, with cane area around 151,305 hectares (Vietnam News, 2024; Vietnam Sugar Association, 2023). Key supply risks are commodity price swings in sugar, aluminium and PET resin — aluminium tariff moves have pushed some brands toward PET (Husky, 2024) — plus climate and salinity pressure on water in the Mekong Delta."}</p>
