        <p>{"Global beverage multinationals deliberately split their value chain to balance control and local reach. The high-value, secret steps — concentrate and syrup production — are centralised (Coca-Cola from Atlanta, PepsiCo from the United States), while bottling, distribution and selling are done locally through franchised or company-owned bottlers (The Coca-Cola Company, 2024). Localisation adapts the product to each market: substituting local cane sugar for HFCS depending on cost and tax, and tailoring flavours and brands to local taste — the energy drink Sting is a major seller in Vietnam. On trade flows, Vietnam sources water, sugar (partly), glass and some PET domestically, while aluminium sheet for cans, certain flavouring agents and much high-speed machinery are imported, leaving the chain exposed to currency and tariff swings. Vietnamese participation is real: Tan Hiep Phat is a fully local, vertically integrated player producing 1 billion+ litres a year (THP, 2024); Masan Consumer (Wake-Up 247) and URC Vietnam (C2 tea) also participate; and contract manufacturing lets smaller brands rent capacity. A Porter's Diamond read of why Vietnam attracts beverage FDI — competitive labour, a young growing middle class, strong GDP growth, and ASEAN free-trade access — is borne out by FDI inflows averaging ~4.8% of GDP over 2015-23, exceeding ASEAN peers (OECD Economic Survey Viet Nam, 2025; Vietnam-Briefing, 2024). These conditions are why global beverage giants keep expanding plants here, and why their MT programmes are growing."}</p>
